  <details class="fold" id="explain">
    <summary><h2>What explains the differences</h2><span class="fold-hint">Show</span></summary>
    <div class="fold-body"><div class="fig">
      <div class="ex-lead">
        <p>The poverty gap closed per dollar is high when administration takes little, most of the rest reaches the poor, and little of that spills over the poverty line. Reaching the poor depends on the country’s poverty rate, since a transfer to everyone reaches the poor in that proportion, and on whom the programme pays and how much.</p>
        <p>The bars split the variation in the poverty gap closed per dollar across the 54 programmes that close some of it (technically, the variance of its logarithm). The parts of each bar add up to 100%. A negative part, left of zero, makes the differences smaller, so the other parts add up to more. Administration’s part is negative in the first two bars because cost shares are higher in poorer countries, where a dollar closes more of the gap.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ex-head"><h3>Contribution to the differences in the poverty gap closed per dollar</h3><button class="hlink" type="button" data-drawer="differ">Method</button></div>
      <div class="keyrow" id="ex-key"></div>
      <div id="ex-list"></div>
      <div class="notes"><p class="notes-label">Notes and sources</p><p>54 programmes that close some of the poverty gap. Percentages are rounded. The 95% intervals are in the tooltips and the Method panel. Source: Figure 2 and Table C5.</p></div></div></div>
  </details>

